---
title: Sweet Potato & Spinach Egg Bake
source: claude
servings: 6
tags: [breakfast, meatless, vegetarian, freezer]
---

> A make-ahead breakfast that stays moist when reheated. About 335 kcal, 21 g protein, 18 g carbs, 3 g fiber and 19 g fat per serving.

Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C). Toss @sweet potato{500%g}(peeled, cut into 1/2-inch cubes) with @olive oil{1%tbsp} and a pinch of the salt, then spread on a #sheet pan{}. Roast until tender and lightly browned, about ~{20%minutes}. Lower the oven to 350°F (175°C).

Wilt @spinach{150%g}(fresh) in a dry #skillet{} over medium heat for ~{3%minutes}. Let it cool slightly, squeeze out as much water as you can and chop it. This keeps leftovers from turning watery.

Whisk @egg{12}(large) with @cottage cheese{225%g}(4%), @salt{1%tsp}, @black pepper{0.5%tsp} and @garlic powder{0.5%tsp}. Blending gives a smoother texture, but whisking works fine.

Grease a #9x13-inch baking dish{}. Spread the sweet potato and spinach evenly in the dish, pour the egg mixture over them and top with @cheddar cheese{85%g}(sharp, shredded).

Bake at 350°F for ~{32%minutes} (30–35), until the center is just set (160°F / 71°C). Pull it slightly early rather than late, because overbaked eggs turn rubbery when reheated.

Let it cool for ~{10%minutes}, cut into 6 pieces and refrigerate in containers once it reaches room temperature.

> Storage: keeps 4 days in the fridge or 2 months in the freezer (thaw overnight). To reheat, microwave a portion covered at 70% power for 60–90 seconds, or warm at 350°F for about 10 minutes.

> For more protein (about 28 g per serving), add 225 g cooked turkey sausage (no longer meatless).
